sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R1-R4, 5 (swamp role SSH identity), round 2 bugs (server admin password by its own vault key, cleanup on pass or fail). docs/reference/database-servers.md#add-a-user.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 6, 22 (WAL read before any change; login and grants in one SQL batch on stdin; the user reads back its grants; no copied scripts or cleanup).
  %% status: draft
  %% command: database:user:create
  %% kind: database_user.create
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Node 12<br/>beast-mysql server
  CLI->>GW: database:user:create shop<br/>--username=reporting
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `printf '%s' "$PASSWORD" | orbit database:user:create shop --username=reporting`
  %%> - syntax: `<slug> --username=USER [--read-only] [--replication] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the connection slug of a database on a MySQL or PostgreSQL server
  %%> - the username: a 1-32 character identifier
  %%> - the password from piped standard input, or a hidden `Password` prompt on a terminal; never an argument
  %%> Grants:
  %%> - `--read-only`: `SELECT` on the database
  %%> - none: all database privileges on MySQL, data-write grants on PostgreSQL
  %%> - `--replication`: PostgreSQL only, `LOGIN REPLICATION` with read-only grants
  Note over GW: Validate, check conflicts<br/>task #5123 · lock beast-mysql
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12
  %%> - connection `shop` has a Database server (`database.server_required`)
  %%> - the server is MySQL or PostgreSQL (`database.driver_unsupported`); on Valkey each user is its own connection
  %%> - `reporting` is not the server's admin login or a system role, such as `root`, `postgres` or `pg_*` (`database.user_reserved`)
  %%> - `reporting` is not the owner of a connection on the server, nor a user another connection recorded (`database.name_conflict`)
  %%> - no database removal runs on `beast-mysql`; the Gateway holds one lock per Database server
  %%> - no other user create runs on `beast-mysql` (`operation.target_busy`), so two creates of one new name never pass the checks together
  %%> Result:
  %%> - task #5123 recorded with the database, owner, username and grant policy
  %%> - the password stored encrypted; nothing has happened on the Node yet
  opt --read-only
    Note over GW: Select reader grants
    %%> What it changes:
    %%> - Replace default write grants with reader grants in the apply batch; the end check verifies them.
  end
  GW->>SW: Store the batches<br/>database-user-create-apply-5123
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - puts three whole values into the Swamp vault on Node 209 (R3)
  %%> - `database-user-create-wal-5123`: the admin password line, then `SHOW wal_level`
  %%> - `database-user-create-apply-5123`: the admin password line, then the batch with the user's password (a SCRAM verifier on PostgreSQL)
  %%> - `database-user-create-check-5123`: the user's password line, then the check
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-database-user-create
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the driver, database, owner, username, grant policy and replication flag
  %%> - the vault key names `database-user-create-wal-5123`, `database-user-create-apply-5123` and `database-user-create-check-5123`
  %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5123
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task number, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
  opt --replication
    Note over GW: Select replication grants
    %%> What it changes:
    %%> - PostgreSQL only: set LOGIN REPLICATION and reader grants, including owner defaults for future public tables.
    %%> - Require logical WAL before applying the batch; without the flag, PostgreSQL gets NOREPLICATION.
    SW->>N: Check logical WAL<br/>docker exec -i · SHOW
    %%> How:
    %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over SSH from 209 with `@swamp/ssh` and the `swamp` role's SSH identity; nothing is copied to the Node
    %%> - one `docker exec -i … psql`; the first stdin line is the admin password, then `SHOW wal_level`
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - reads the live WAL level before any change
    %%> Refuses:
    %%> - another level than `logical`: the next steps skip, and the Gateway fails the task with `database.wal_level_not_logical`; nothing changes
    %%> Not included:
    %%> - Orbit never changes the server configuration
  end
  SW->>N: Apply login and grants<br/>docker exec -i · SQL on stdin
  %%> How:
  %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over SSH from 209 with `@swamp/ssh` and the `swamp` role's SSH identity; nothing is copied to the Node
  %%> - one `docker exec -i`; inside the container the first stdin line is the admin password, read into the client's password variable, then the batch; never in argv or a file
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - creates the login, or sets the password of a user Orbit recorded before
  %%> - PostgreSQL: adds or removes the replication attribute
  %%> - replaces the user's privileges with exactly the requested ones, in the same batch
  %%> Refuses:
  %%> - an existing role or account (MySQL: any host) that this connection did not record (`database.name_conflict`); the batch stops before any change and prints the code for the Gateway
  %%> - MySQL: escapes underscores, so the grant covers only `shop`
  %%> Not included:
  %%> - Orbit never changes the server configuration or creates publications
  SW->>N: Check the user<br/>docker exec -i · login, grants
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the user logs in over TCP with its own password, from the first stdin line, and reads back its name, attributes and grants
  %%> - MySQL: `SHOW GRANTS` lists exactly the requested grant on its database
  %%> - PostgreSQL: the replication attribute, connect and schema use, and the owner's default table privileges for the user
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the user only counts as created when the server shows it; the Gateway compares the answer in the native result
  alt every step passes
    N-->>SW: login and grants match
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the check passed
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record the user<br/>grants · task succeeded
    %%> Result:
    %%> - user `reporting` recorded for `shop`, with its privileges and encrypted password
    %%> - `database:user:list shop` shows it; `vault:sync` can publish its password to Vaultwarden
    %%> - task #5123 succeeds
  else a step fails
    N--xSW: conflict or exit ≠ 0
    %%> When:
    %%> - the wrong WAL level, a name conflict, failed SQL, other grants, or a lost SSH connection
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed and its error code
    Note over GW: Keep the records<br/>task failed · rerun after a fix
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the user records stay as before; a new user is not recorded
    %%> - task #5123 fails
    %%> On the Node:
    %%> - a login created before the failure stays; there is no rollback
    %%> - that is safe: a rerun sets the password and replaces the grants again
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause; it is a new task
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5123
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
  end
